    "narrative": "The anchor of our lineup today is undoubtedly **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, who boasts an elite 80.31 efficiency score against Montero. He is the clear focal point of the offense, benefiting from a favorable +12% bump due to the SP's expected ERA of 4.51 and an advantageous spot in the #2 hole. Beyond **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, we are heavily leaning into the favorable conditions surrounding the Arizona-Colorado contest; **Luis Arraez**, **Matt Chapman**, and **Drew Gilbert** all gain momentum from the matchup against Nelson. **Luis Arraez** leads this secondary tier with a 63.57 score, bolstered by his prime spot in the batting order, while **Will Smith** remains a stable play at 63.24 despite a moderately difficult matchup against Canning.\n\nWe are forced to navigate a few tighter margins elsewhere. **Gavin Sheets** sits at a precarious 42.96, barely clearing our floor, largely because the matchup against Sheehan acts as a significant drag on his ceiling. Our most difficult decision was keeping **Taylor Ward** on the bench with a 39.23 score. While his leadoff spot provided a +10% boost, the confluence of a weak park factor and a -19% penalty against the SP\u2019s skill level pushed him just below our 40.0 cutoff. Meanwhile, we continue to monitor the status of **Cal Raleigh** and **Yainer Diaz**, both of whom remain sidelined on the IL, leaving us thin at the back end of the roster. We are currently cycling through depth options like **Tyler Freeman**, **Dylan Beavers**, **Masyn Winn**, **Jeremiah Jackson**, **Brendan Donovan**, **Jung Hoo Lee**, and **Gleyber Torres** to maximize our daily utility, though none challenged the primary starters for a position today. We are prioritizing stability, but keep a close eye on **Jonathan Aranda** and **Bryson Stott**; both face elite arms in Bradish and Ch. Burns, making them candidates for late-inning volatility.",
